<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladivostok</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladivostok is a Russian port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Founded in 1860 as a military outpost after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gained the territory from China, it became the Pacific terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> and the base of the Pacific Fleet. In the early 20th century it had a large Korean community that was active in the independence movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladivostok.jpg" alt="Vladivostok" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vladivostok._GUM_Department_Store_P8070703_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vladivostok stands around the Golden Horn Bay on the southern extremity of the Muravyov-Amursky Peninsula, about 45 kilometres from the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and 134 kilometres from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Its name means Lord of the East or Ruler of the East, from the Slavic word for to rule and the Russian word for east. The area was historically part of Outer Manchuria. Some modern Chinese historians claim a Chinese settlement stood on the site around 600 CE under the name Yongmingcheng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gained the territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> under the Treaty of Aigun, confirmed by the Convention of Peking, and the city was founded as a military outpost on 2 July 1860. The first civilian settler, the merchant Yakov Lazarevich Semyonov, arrived in 1861. In the early 1870s the main naval base of the Siberian Military Flotilla was moved there from Nikolaevsk-on-Amur, which set off a construction boom. The first urban plan, by Mikhail Lubensky, dates from 1868, and the railway station was completed in 1894. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a>, finished in 1905, linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> with Vladivostok, Russia's most important Pacific port.</p><p>From the late 1890s to the early 1920s half of the population was Asian, the Chinese being the largest group, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and Japanese. The Chinese quarter, called Millionka, held up to 50,000 people at its peak. Koreans moved to the area in large numbers after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910, and by 1915 about 10,000 lived in the city. Their enclave, Sinhanch'on, became a hub of the Korean independence movement and hosted the first Korean provisional government, the Korean Independence Army Government. On the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> both quarters were liquidated and their residents deported between 1936 and 1938.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a>, White Russian and Allied forces occupied Vladivostok in 1918. The last of them, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, were not withdrawn until 1922, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the city the same year. By then it was in decline, and its population had fallen to 106,000. Under the New Economic Policy of 1923 to 1925 the commercial port reopened and became the most profitable in the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vladivostok is the administrative centre of Primorsky Krai and the largest Russian port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. It is the home of the headquarters of the Pacific Fleet of the Russian Navy and the starting point of the Trans-Siberian portion of the Eurasian Land Bridge. Its population passed 500,000 in the 1970s and reached a high of over 648,000 in 1992. More than 90 per cent of residents declare Russian ethnicity, though minorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and Chinese remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vladivostok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protests followed in Ussuriysk and in Sinhanch’on in <b>Vladivostok</b>, and Koreans in Hawaii and Philadelphia held meetings.</blockquote><a href="/movement/march-first-movement">March First Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The song travelled far: in 1906 the American consul in <b>Vladivostok</b>, Richard T. Greener, reported Russian soldiers singing it during the Revolution of 1905, and in 1945 it was better known than the Labour Party's own anthem at Labour rallies in Crewe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote>Rival groups in Seoul and <b>Vladivostok</b> merged with it later that year, and Syngman Rhee was chosen as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
